"In Unit 1 you built the ideas a computer runs on: switches, numbers, codes, logic gates, and an adder. Now it is time to open the box and see where those ideas live. You might expect something mysterious inside. What you find is a small team of parts. Each part has one job, and one big board holds them all together.": "4373f35a5c7c",
"In Unit 1 you built the ideas a computer runs on: switches, numbers, codes, logic gates, and an adder. Now it is time to open the box and see where those ideas live. You might expect something mysterious inside. What you find is a small team of parts. Each part has one job, and one big board holds them all together. That board is the motherboard. It is the largest board inside the case, and every other part plugs into it. Thin copper lines run across it and connect the parts, so that they can pass data to one another. The motherboard does not do any calculating itself. Its job is to connect. The most important part plugged into the motherboard is the processor, also called the CPU. It carries out the instructions of programs, one after another. All the adding and deciding you saw in Lessons 3 and 4 happens here, on one small chip. Its transistors turn electrical energy into heat as they work, so a block of metal fins and a fan sit on top of the processor to carry the heat away. A computer has two places to keep things, and they are easy to mix up. Memory, or RAM, holds the programs and data that are in use right now. It is fast, but it loses everything when the power is turned off. Storage keeps your programs and files even when the computer is off. In many computers today the storage is a solid-state drive, which keeps data in chips and has no moving parts. Others use a hard disk, with disks that spin. Two more parts finish the team. The graphics card draws the picture that you see, working out the color of every pixel. In many computers this job is done by graphics built into the processor, with no separate card. The power supply takes electricity from the wall socket and changes it into the low voltages that the other parts need. Now watch the team work. When you open an app, it is copied from storage into memory. The processor takes its instructions from memory and carries them out. The graphics card draws the result as a picture for the screen. The power supply keeps every part running, and the motherboard connects them all. A laptop has the same kinds of parts, only smaller and packed closer together. Some chips even combine the processor, the graphics, and more in a single piece, called a system on a chip.": "ace1ad67d2fd",
